/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/ai-102.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("ai-102", [{"t":"Choosing the right Azure AI service for a workload: Azure OpenAI, AI Search, Vision, Language, Speech, Translator and Document Intelligence","body":["An Azure AI engineer's first job is picking the right tool. Azure offers a family of prebuilt AI services, each trained by Microsoft for one kind of problem, plus Azure OpenAI and the wider model catalog for generative AI. Many AI-102 questions describe a business need in a sentence or two and ask which service fits, so you need a clear mental map of what each one does and, just as important, what it does not do.","Azure OpenAI (reached through Azure AI Foundry) hosts large language models for chat, summarization, code, reasoning and embeddings, plus image generation models. Use it when the output is generated content or when you need flexible reasoning over text. Azure AI Search indexes your content for keyword, vector and hybrid search, and is the usual retrieval layer for retrieval augmented generation (RAG). Azure AI Vision analyzes images: captions, tags, objects, people and text through its Read (optical character recognition, OCR) feature. Custom Vision trains your own image classifiers and object detectors, and Azure AI Face detects faces and, with approval, identifies people.","Azure AI Language covers text analytics (sentiment, key phrases, entities, personally identifiable information detection), conversational language understanding (CLU), custom question answering and custom text classification. Azure AI Speech converts speech to text and text to speech, translates speech and recognizes wake words. Azure AI Translator translates text and whole documents. Azure AI Document Intelligence extracts text, tables and named fields from forms such as invoices, receipts and ID documents, and trains custom extraction models. Azure AI Content Understanding is a newer service that extracts fields from documents, images, audio and video with one schema.","The exam likes near misses. Reading text from a photo of a sign is Vision Read, but pulling the total and due date from an invoice is Document Intelligence, because you want named fields, not just lines of text. Detecting that a review is negative is Language sentiment analysis, but deciding whether a post contains hate or violence is Azure AI Content Safety. Translating a chat message is Translator; translating a live speaker is Speech translation. When a prebuilt capability covers the need, prefer it over training a custom model or writing a prompt, because it is cheaper, faster and already evaluated.","Finally, remember the generative option. A large language model can often do sentiment or extraction too, but a purpose-built service is usually cheaper, more predictable and easier to evaluate. Choose Azure OpenAI when the task needs open-ended generation, reasoning or conversation."],"terms":[["Azure AI services","Microsoft's family of prebuilt AI APIs for vision, language, speech, translation, documents and content safety, called over REST or SDKs."],["Azure OpenAI","Azure-hosted OpenAI models for chat, reasoning, embeddings and image generation, deployed and managed through Azure AI Foundry."],["Document Intelligence","A service that extracts text, structure and named fields from documents using prebuilt or custom models."],["Content Safety","A service that detects harmful text and images across hate, sexual, violence and self-harm categories."]],"example":"An insurance company wants to process claim forms, let agents search past claims in plain language and read summaries aloud to visually impaired staff. The engineer uses Document Intelligence to extract fields from the forms, Azure AI Search with vector and keyword indexing for retrieval, Azure OpenAI to summarize claims, and Speech text to speech to read the summaries.","tip":"Match the output the scenario asks for: named fields from forms means Document Intelligence, plain text lines means Vision Read, harmful-content scores means Content Safety, and generated prose means Azure OpenAI.","check":[["A company wants to know whether customer reviews are positive or negative. Which service is the most direct fit?","Azure AI Language sentiment analysis. It is purpose-built, returns scores per sentence and is cheaper and more predictable than prompting a large language model."],["Which service would you use to find the text on a street sign in a photo, and which to get the total from a receipt?","Azure AI Vision's Read (OCR) feature for the sign; Document Intelligence's prebuilt receipt model for the receipt total, because it returns a named field."]]},{"t":"Azure AI Foundry hubs, projects and resources vs single-service and multi-service Azure AI services resources","body":["Before you can call any AI service you need an Azure resource that gives you an endpoint, credentials and a bill. The exam expects you to know the kinds of resources and when each is the right choice. Azure has renamed this area several times: Azure AI Studio became Azure AI Foundry and later Microsoft Foundry, and the resource once called Cognitive Services is now Azure AI services. The resource type in templates is still `Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts`, so you will see the old name in code.","A single-service resource provides one service, such as Language, Speech or Custom Vision, with its own endpoint and keys. It is useful when you want a Free (F0) tier for that one service, separate billing per service, or keys that can only call one API. A multi-service Azure AI services resource exposes many services (Vision, Language, Speech, Translator, Document Intelligence and more) behind one endpoint and key pair with one bill. It is the simplest choice when one app uses several services, and it is the resource you attach to an Azure AI Search skillset to pay for AI enrichment.","Azure AI Foundry is the portal and platform for building generative AI apps and agents. A Foundry project is the workspace where you deploy models, build agents, run evaluations and use playgrounds. Projects can live on an Azure AI Foundry resource (an Azure AI services resource that also supports projects), which is the simpler, newer setup. The older hub-based setup uses an Azure AI hub, which is built on Azure Machine Learning: the hub holds shared settings such as connections to other resources, networking, compute and security, and teams create projects under it. A hub also brings dependent resources such as a storage account and a Key Vault.","Choose based on sharing and isolation. If several teams need common connections and governance but separate workspaces, create one hub (or one Foundry resource) with a project per team or app. If an app only needs to call one or two prebuilt APIs, a single-service or multi-service resource without Foundry is enough. Connections in a project store how to reach other resources, such as an Azure AI Search service or a storage account, so developers do not paste keys into code.","Whatever you create, you get an endpoint URL and, unless key access is disabled, two keys. Resources are created in a region, and not every service or model is available in every region, which is the next topic."],"terms":[["Single-service resource","An Azure resource for one AI service, with its own endpoint, keys, pricing tier and bill."],["Multi-service resource","An Azure AI services resource that exposes many AI services behind one endpoint and key pair with combined billing."],["Hub","A shared Azure AI Foundry container, built on Azure Machine Learning, that holds connections, security and compute for several projects."],["Project","A Foundry workspace where you deploy models, build agents, evaluate and manage the assets for one app or team."]],"example":"A consultancy runs three client pilots. It creates one hub with private networking and a connection to a shared Azure AI Search service, then a project per client so each team's deployments, prompts and evaluations stay separate while governance is set once.","tip":"If the question stresses one endpoint and one bill for several prebuilt services, pick the multi-service resource. If it stresses a Free tier or separate keys per service, pick single-service resources. Shared governance with separate workspaces means a hub with projects.","check":[["An app uses Vision, Language and Translator, and finance wants one bill. What should you create?","A multi-service Azure AI services resource, which gives one endpoint, one key pair and combined billing."],["What does a hub provide that a project alone does not?","Shared settings for many projects: connections to other resources, networking, compute and security policies, plus the dependent storage and Key Vault."]]},{"t":"Planning model deployments: regions, model availability, deployment types (Standard, Global, Data Zone, Provisioned) and quotas","body":["In Azure OpenAI you do not call a model directly. You create a deployment: a named instance of a specific model and version in your resource, with its own capacity. Your code calls the deployment name, not the model name, which lets you swap versions behind the same name. Planning a deployment means choosing a region, a model that is available there, a deployment type and how much quota to give it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deploying Azure AI resources with the portal, Azure CLI, ARM templates and Bicep","body":["You can create every Azure AI resource by clicking through the Azure portal or the Foundry portal, and that is a good way to learn. In production, though, teams deploy with scripts and infrastructure as code so environments are repeatable, reviewed and easy to rebuild. The exam expects you to recognize the main options and read a short template."],"locked":true},{"t":"Endpoints, keys and SDKs: calling Azure AI services with REST and the Python and C# SDKs","body":["Every Azure AI service is ultimately a REST API. Your app sends an HTTPS request to the resource's endpoint with a path for the operation, an `api-version` query parameter, a credential and usually a JSON body. The SDKs for Python, C#, JavaScript and Java wrap these calls so you work with typed objects instead of raw JSON, but knowing the REST shape helps you debug and answer exam questions that show a request."],"locked":true},{"t":"Authentication: API keys vs Microsoft Entra ID, managed identities and Cognitive Services RBAC roles","body":["Azure AI services accept two kinds of credentials. Keys are simple: every resource has two, and anyone who holds one can call the resource. That simplicity is also the risk, because a key copied into code, a config file or a chat message works for whoever finds it, and the logs cannot tell you which person or app used it. Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory) authentication replaces the shared secret with a token issued to a specific identity, and access is controlled with Azure role-based access control (RBAC)."],"locked":true},{"t":"Protecting keys and networks: Azure Key Vault, key rotation, private endpoints and network restrictions","body":["Even when you plan to move to Microsoft Entra ID, many apps still use keys, and every resource is reachable over the network. Securing an Azure AI solution means protecting the secrets and limiting who can even reach the endpoint."],"locked":true},{"t":"Running Azure AI services in containers: connected and disconnected containers and billing settings","body":["Some Azure AI capabilities are available as Docker containers that you run on your own hardware, in Azure Kubernetes Service, Azure Container Instances or at the edge. Containers bring the model close to the data, which helps when data must stay on premises for compliance, when latency to the cloud is too high, or when you need to control throughput yourself. Containers are available for selected features, including parts of Language (such as sentiment and language detection), Speech to text and text to speech, Translator, Vision Read and Document Intelligence. Check which features are offered before planning."],"locked":true},{"t":"Monitoring and cost: Azure Monitor metrics, diagnostic logs, alerts, pricing tiers and budgets","body":["Once an AI solution is live you need to know whether it is healthy, how it is used and what it costs. Azure AI resources plug into Azure Monitor like other Azure services, and the exam expects you to choose between metrics, logs and alerts, and to understand pricing tiers."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI principles and Azure AI Content Safety: harm categories, severity levels and blocklists","body":["Microsoft builds its AI services around six responsible AI principles, and the exam expects you to recognize them in scenarios. Fairness: AI systems should treat all people fairly and not disadvantage groups. Reliability and safety: they should perform consistently and safely, including in unexpected conditions. Privacy and security: they should protect data and respect privacy. Inclusiveness: they should empower everyone, including people with disabilities. Transparency: people should understand that they are dealing with AI, how it works and its limits. Accountability: people remain responsible for how AI systems are designed and used."],"locked":true},{"t":"Content filters, prompt shields and groundedness detection for generative AI applications","body":["Generative AI apps face risks that classic moderation does not cover: users trying to break the model's rules, malicious instructions hidden in documents, and answers that sound confident but are not supported by the sources. Azure provides several layered guardrails for these, and AI-102 expects you to match each risk to the right control."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure OpenAI and the Azure AI Foundry model catalog: chat, reasoning, embedding and image models, and how to choose and deploy one","body":["Generative AI on Azure starts with choosing a model. The Azure AI Foundry model catalog lists models from OpenAI (sold as Azure OpenAI), Microsoft and other providers such as Meta, Mistral and Cohere. OpenAI models and some others are deployed into your Azure OpenAI or Foundry resource; many third-party models are offered as serverless APIs billed per token, and some can be deployed to managed compute that you pay for by the hour. Each catalog entry has a model card describing its purpose, context length, supported regions and deployment options."],"locked":true},{"t":"Chat completions: system, user and assistant messages, and calling a deployment with the Azure OpenAI SDK","body":["Most generative AI apps use the chat completions API. You send a list of messages, and the model returns the next assistant message. Each message has a role. The system message (in newer models sometimes called the developer message) sets the model's behavior: persona, tone, rules, what topics to stay within, what to do when unsure and the output format. User messages are what the person typed. Assistant messages are the model's earlier replies. You can also include tool messages that return the results of function calls."],"locked":true},{"t":"Tuning model output with parameters: temperature, top_p, max tokens, stop sequences and penalties","body":["A language model generates text one token at a time. At each step it produces a probability for every possible next token and then samples one. Request parameters change how that sampling works and when generation stops, so the same prompt can produce focused, repeatable answers or varied, creative ones. The exam expects you to choose the right parameter for a described behavior."],"locked":true},{"t":"Prompt engineering techniques: clear instructions, few-shot examples, output formats and step-by-step reasoning","body":["Prompt engineering is the practice of writing inputs that reliably get the output you need. It is the cheapest way to improve a generative AI app, so it comes before RAG or fine-tuning. The model only knows what is in its training and in the prompt, and it cannot ask you what you meant, so ambiguity in the prompt becomes inconsistency in the output."],"locked":true},{"t":"Retrieval augmented generation (RAG): grounding a model in your own data with Azure AI Search","body":["A language model knows only what was in its training data, which is out of date and does not include your company's private documents. Asked about them, it may invent a plausible answer. Retrieval augmented generation (RAG) fixes this by retrieving relevant passages from your own data at question time and adding them to the prompt, with instructions to answer only from those passages. The model's job changes from remembering facts to reading and summarizing the facts you hand it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Embeddings and vector retrieval for RAG: chunking documents, embedding models and similarity search","body":["Keyword search finds documents that share words with the query. That fails when users describe the same idea with different words: a search for 'car won't start' may miss a passage about 'engine fails to turn over'. Vector search solves this by comparing meaning. An embedding model turns a piece of text into a vector, a list of hundreds or thousands of numbers, placed so that texts with similar meaning have vectors that are close together."],"locked":true},{"t":"Prompt flow in Azure AI Foundry: flows, nodes, connections, variants and deployment","body":["Real generative AI apps are more than one prompt. A RAG chat app might rewrite the question, embed it, search an index, format the results, call the model and post-process the answer. Prompt flow is a development tool in Azure AI Foundry (and Azure Machine Learning) for building, testing, evaluating and deploying these multi-step pipelines as a visual graph backed by files you can keep in source control."],"locked":true},{"t":"Evaluating generative AI apps: groundedness, relevance, coherence, fluency and safety evaluations","body":["Generative AI output is not simply right or wrong, and small prompt changes can improve one kind of question while breaking another. Evaluation means running your app against a set of test inputs and scoring the outputs systematically, so decisions about prompts, models and retrieval are based on evidence rather than a few chats in the playground. Azure AI Foundry provides built-in evaluators and an evaluation dashboard, and the Azure AI Evaluation SDK lets you run the same evaluators in code or in a pipeline."],"locked":true},{"t":"Fine-tuning vs prompt engineering vs RAG: when each approach fits and how fine-tuning data is prepared","body":["When a base model does not do what you want, there are three main ways to improve it, and the exam often asks which to use. They solve different problems and are often combined, so think about what is actually wrong."],"locked":true},{"t":"Generating images and working with multimodal chat models that accept images","body":["Generative AI is not limited to text. Two capabilities appear on the exam: creating images from text, and sending images to a chat model so it can reason about them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Operating generative AI apps: token usage, rate limits, latency, tracing and monitoring","body":["Getting a prototype to answer well is half the work; running it for thousands of users is the other half. Operating a generative AI app means controlling cost, staying within rate limits, keeping latency acceptable and being able to see why a given answer was produced."],"locked":true},{"t":"What an AI agent is: model, instructions and tools, and when an agent fits better than a plain chat app","body":["An AI agent is software that uses a language model to pursue a goal on a user's behalf by deciding what to do next and taking actions through tools. A plain chat app sends a prompt and shows the reply. An agent runs a loop: it reads the request, decides whether it needs information or an action, calls a tool, looks at the result, and repeats until it can give a final answer or complete the task."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Foundry Agent Service: agents, threads, messages and runs","body":["Azure AI Foundry Agent Service is a managed service for building and hosting agents. It handles the parts that are tedious to build yourself: storing conversation state, calling tools, managing files and vector stores, applying content filters and tracing. You define the agent, and the service runs the loop of model calls and tool calls. You can create agents in the Agents page of a Foundry project or in code with the Azure AI Projects or Agents SDKs for Python, C# and other languages."],"locked":true},{"t":"Agent tools: file search, code interpreter, Azure AI Search, OpenAPI and function calling","body":["Tools are what make an agent more than a chatbot. Azure AI Foundry Agent Service offers built-in tools that the service runs for you, and ways to connect your own code and APIs. Choosing the right tool for a scenario is a common exam task."],"locked":true},{"t":"Function calling: describing functions with JSON schema and handling tool calls in code","body":["Function calling (also called tool calling) is the mechanism that lets a language model use your code. The model never runs anything itself. You describe the functions it may use; when it decides one would help, it returns a structured request naming the function and its arguments, your code runs the function, and you send the result back so the model can continue. This works both in plain chat completions and inside Agent Service."],"locked":true},{"t":"Building agents in code with Semantic Kernel, AutoGen and the Microsoft Agent Framework","body":["Agent Service hosts agents for you, but many teams also build agent logic in their own code with an open-source framework. Frameworks give you abstractions for models, tools, memory and multi-agent patterns, and let you run agents inside your own app or service. The AI-102 objectives name Semantic Kernel and AutoGen, and Microsoft has since introduced the Microsoft Agent Framework, which brings the two together."],"locked":true},{"t":"Multi-agent solutions: orchestration patterns, handoffs and connected agents","body":["A single agent with many tools and long instructions becomes hard to steer: it picks the wrong tool, forgets rules and is difficult to test. Multi-agent solutions split the work among specialized agents, each with focused instructions and a small set of tools, and coordinate them. This mirrors how teams of people divide work."],"locked":true},{"t":"Testing, securing and monitoring agents: tracing, human approval and least-privilege tools","body":["Agents act, so their failures have consequences beyond a bad paragraph: a wrong refund, a leaked record, an email to the wrong person. Testing, security and monitoring must be designed in from the start."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deploying agents and integrating them into applications","body":["An agent that works in the playground still needs to reach users. Deployment means making the agent available to applications with proper identity, networking, versioning and operations, and integration means connecting it to the channels and systems where users already work."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Vision Image Analysis: captions, dense captions, tags, objects, people and smart crops","body":["Azure AI Vision's Image Analysis API describes what is in an image using prebuilt models trained by Microsoft, with no training on your part. You send an image (as a URL or as binary data) and a list of visual features you want, and the service returns JSON with results and confidence scores. The current version, Image Analysis 4.0, is called at the `imageanalysis:analyze` path with a `features` query parameter, or through the `ImageAnalysisClient` in the Python and C# SDKs."],"locked":true},{"t":"Reading printed and handwritten text with the OCR (Read) feature of Azure AI Vision","body":["Optical character recognition (OCR) turns text in images into machine-readable text. Azure AI Vision provides it through the Read feature, which recognizes printed and handwritten text in many languages in photos, screenshots and scans: street signs, product labels, whiteboards, posters and handwritten notes."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom Vision: image classification (multiclass vs multilabel) vs object detection","body":["Prebuilt Image Analysis knows thousands of general concepts but not your specific ones: your product range, your defect types, your plant species. Azure AI Custom Vision lets you train your own image models from your own labeled images with little machine learning knowledge, using a web portal or SDK. Microsoft has announced a future retirement for Custom Vision and points new projects toward other options, so check its current status, but it remains part of the AI-102 objectives."],"locked":true},{"t":"Training and evaluating a Custom Vision model: tagging images, iterations, precision, recall and mAP","body":["Training a Custom Vision model is a loop: collect images, tag them, train, evaluate, and improve the data. The model's quality depends far more on the images than on any setting."],"locked":true},{"t":"Publishing and consuming a Custom Vision model: prediction resource, published iterations and exporting compact models","body":["A trained iteration is not yet available to your app. You make it available by publishing it to a prediction resource, or by exporting it to run somewhere else. Knowing this step explains a classic exam scenario: the model was retrained, but the app still behaves the same."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Face: face detection, attributes and Limited Access features","body":["Azure AI Face analyzes human faces in images. Because face technology can affect privacy and civil liberties, it is one of the most tightly governed Azure AI services, and the exam tests both what it can do and what it restricts."],"locked":true},{"t":"Analyzing video with Azure AI Video Indexer","body":["Video is hard to search: an hour of footage can hide the one sentence or scene you need. Azure AI Video Indexer analyzes video and audio files with many AI models at once and produces time-coded insights that make video searchable, accessible and easier to edit or moderate."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing between prebuilt Image Analysis, Custom Vision and multimodal models for a vision task","body":["Azure gives you several ways to get answers from images, and many exam scenarios are really asking you to choose among them. The main options are prebuilt Image Analysis, a custom model trained with Custom Vision, a multimodal chat model, and specialist services such as Face, Document Intelligence and Video Indexer."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Language text analysis: language detection, key phrases, entities, entity linking and sentiment with opinion mining","body":["Azure AI Language offers prebuilt natural language processing (NLP) features that analyze text without any training. You send documents (plain text with an id and optionally a language code) and choose the kind of analysis. The REST call posts to the `language/:analyze-text` path with a `kind` such as `LanguageDetection`, `KeyPhraseExtraction`, `EntityRecognition`, `EntityLinking` or `SentimentAnalysis`; the SDKs expose methods such as `detect_language` and `analyze_sentiment`. Results come back per document, with errors reported per document so one bad input does not fail the batch. Larger jobs, and combining several analyses in one request, use the asynchronous analyze-text jobs endpoint."],"locked":true},{"t":"Detecting and redacting personally identifiable information (PII) with Azure AI Language","body":["Organizations handle text full of personal data: chat transcripts, emails, support tickets, medical notes. Storing, sharing or sending that text to other systems creates privacy and compliance risk. Azure AI Language's PII detection finds personally identifiable information in text and can return a redacted copy, helping you meet regulations such as GDPR and internal data handling policies."],"locked":true},{"t":"Translating text and documents with Azure AI Translator and Custom Translator","body":["Azure AI Translator translates text between more than 100 languages using neural machine translation. It has three parts you should know: text translation for strings in real time, document translation for whole files, and Custom Translator for training models on your own terminology."],"locked":true},{"t":"Speech to text with Azure AI Speech: real-time, continuous and batch transcription","body":["Azure AI Speech converts spoken audio into text, a capability called speech recognition or speech to text. It supports many languages and powers captions, dictation, voice commands and call-center analytics. Your choice of method depends on whether the audio is live or recorded and how much of it there is."],"locked":true},{"t":"Text to speech with neural voices and SSML for pronunciation, pauses, rate and style","body":["Text to speech (speech synthesis) turns text into natural-sounding audio. Azure AI Speech offers hundreds of prebuilt neural voices across many languages and locales, named like `en-US-AvaMultilingualNeural` or `en-GB-SoniaNeural`. Uses include voice assistants, reading content aloud for accessibility, phone systems and audio versions of articles."],"locked":true},{"t":"Speech translation, intent recognition and keyword recognition with the Speech SDK","body":["Beyond plain transcription and synthesis, the Speech SDK combines recognition with other tasks. Three appear in the AI-102 objectives: translating speech, recognizing what a speaker wants, and listening for a wake word."],"locked":true},{"t":"Conversational language understanding (CLU): intents, entities, utterances, training and deployment","body":["Conversational language understanding (CLU) is the Azure AI Language feature that interprets what a user wants from a natural language command or question. It replaced the older Language Understanding service (LUIS), which has been retired. You build a CLU project in Language Studio (or the Foundry portal) or through the authoring REST API, train a custom model on your examples, deploy it and query it from apps and bots."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom question answering: projects, sources, multi-turn prompts, synonyms and confidence thresholds","body":["Custom question answering, a feature of Azure AI Language, builds a knowledge base of question-and-answer pairs from your existing content and answers users' natural language questions from it. It replaced the retired QnA Maker service. Typical uses are FAQ bots for HR, IT and customer support, where answers should be exactly the approved text rather than generated."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom text classification and custom named entity recognition","body":["Prebuilt Language features know general categories, but businesses often need their own: route tickets into their own queues, find clauses in their contracts, pull fields that only their documents contain. Azure AI Language provides two custom features for this, both trained on your labeled documents: custom text classification and custom named entity recognition (custom NER)."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom speech models and custom neural voice: when to train them and responsible use limits","body":["Prebuilt speech models work well for general conversation, but some situations need customization: recognition that struggles with specialist vocabulary or noisy environments, or a brand that wants its own distinctive synthetic voice. Azure AI Speech offers custom speech for recognition and custom neural voice for synthesis."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Search components: data sources, indexers, indexes, skillsets, service tiers, replicas and partitions","body":["Azure AI Search (formerly Azure Cognitive Search) is a managed search service. It stores your content in indexes and answers keyword, vector and hybrid queries quickly, and it can enrich content with AI as it is imported. It is the backbone of knowledge mining (making large volumes of documents searchable and analyzable) and of retrieval for RAG apps."],"locked":true},{"t":"Designing a search index: key field, field attributes (searchable, filterable, sortable, facetable, retrievable), analyzers and suggesters","body":["An index schema defines the fields of each search document and how each field can be used. Good design decides what users can search, filter, sort and see, and affects storage size and relevance. Some attributes cannot be changed on an existing field without rebuilding the index, so design with the queries in mind."],"locked":true},{"t":"AI enrichment with built-in skills: document cracking, OCR, image analysis, entities and key phrases in a skillset","body":["Raw content is often not searchable as-is: scanned PDFs are images, photos have no text, and long documents bury the people and places mentioned in them. AI enrichment in Azure AI Search uses a skillset, attached to an indexer, to run AI steps on each document during indexing and add the results as new searchable fields."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom skills: calling an Azure Function or web API from a skillset","body":["Built-in skills cover common AI tasks, but many organizations need enrichment only they can provide: classifying documents with their own model, looking up a customer ID in an internal system, extracting a proprietary code format or calling a specialized service. A custom skill plugs your own code into the enrichment pipeline."],"locked":true},{"t":"Querying an index: simple and full Lucene syntax, OData filters, facets, sorting and paging","body":["Queries are sent to an index's `docs/search` endpoint with a POST body or query string, or through the SDK's `search` method on a `SearchClient`, using a query key for read-only access. A query combines several parts, each with its own syntax, and the exam often shows a query and asks what it returns or how to fix it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Semantic ranking, vector search and hybrid queries in Azure AI Search","body":["Classic full-text search ranks results with BM25, a formula based on how often query terms appear in a document and how rare they are. It is fast and excellent for exact terms, but it does not understand meaning. Azure AI Search adds three capabilities that improve relevance, and RAG solutions usually combine them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Knowledge store: projections to tables, objects and files in Azure Storage","body":["AI enrichment produces valuable data: entities, key phrases, sentiment, OCR text, image tags. A search index is built for queries, not for analytics, data science or other applications. A knowledge store saves the enriched output of a skillset into Azure Storage so other tools can use it, independent of the index."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Document Intelligence prebuilt models: read, layout, invoice, receipt and ID document","body":["Azure AI Document Intelligence (formerly Form Recognizer) extracts text, structure and data from documents such as PDFs, scanned images and Office files. Where OCR alone gives you lines of text, Document Intelligence understands documents: paragraphs, tables, checkboxes and named fields like invoice totals. Prebuilt models work immediately without training."],"locked":true},{"t":"Custom Document Intelligence models: template vs neural, custom classifiers and composed models","body":["Prebuilt models cover common documents, but most organizations also have their own forms: an internal claim form, a supplier's purchase order, a lab report. Document Intelligence lets you train custom models on a small number of your own labeled examples to extract exactly the fields you need."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Content Understanding: extracting fields from documents, images, audio and video with analyzers","body":["Azure AI Content Understanding is a newer Azure AI service that turns unstructured content of any type (documents, images, audio and video) into structured output that applications, search indexes and agents can use. It combines capabilities that previously required several services, such as OCR, layout analysis, speech transcription and video analysis, with generative AI models that extract or generate the specific fields you ask for."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-09-25"});
